  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "None noted; the full paper, including appendices, is present.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study evaluates model scaling in video generation, a new modality relative to the language-model scaling claims, but it does not fit or test a power law.",
    "gate_note": "The paper cites language-model scalability as motivation and reports its own video-model size sweep. Its principal scaling results are FVD, not held-out loss; it does not fit scaling exponents, predict larger runs from smaller ones, or identify a compute-optimal parameter/token allocation. Validation-loss curves are shown, but the text gives no fitted loss law. Results from other papers are not recorded.",
    "test_reason": "This is a video-generation method paper. It cites LLM scalability (Kaplan et al.) as motivation and reports its own NBP models' FVD at three sizes (700M, 1.2B, 3B), concluding the method is 'scalable'. It fits no power law, extrapolates nothing and studies no compute-optimal allocation. The size sweep spans under one order of magnitude, and its outcome is FVD, not held-out loss. Validation-loss curves (Fig. 5, Fig. 6) are shown only as figures, with no fit. The scaling results therefore describe the authors' own method's performance, not a test of any version, including V5's claim that the same power-law form holds in video.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that NBP is a more efficient and scalable video-generation framework (about 11× faster, better FVD than NTP), and that FVD improves with model size. They make no power-law or scaling-law claim.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study trains semi-autoregressive video generators from scratch and evaluates video FVD and validation loss. It does not use the original language-model loss-scaling fit or compute-optimal allocation procedure.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Video-generation results vary with block size and block shape; the authors report that block size affects the trade-off between FVD and inference speed.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "NBP FVD falls with model size from 700M to 3B: UCF-101 103.3→85.8→55.3; K600 25.5→23.0→19.5. NTP falls too (37.4→31.4→29.0 at 20 epochs).",
        "proposed_claim": "Video-generation quality (FVD) of autoregressive/semi-AR video transformers improves monotonically with parameter count over 700M–3B.",
